    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>A hail damage roof insurance claim succeeds when you can show the hail caused real, functional damage, you report it promptly, and you understand the parts of your policy that limit roof payments. In Texas, that means checking for cosmetic damage exclusions, actual cash value roof endorsements and wind and hail deductibles before you file.</p>
<p>This guide explains how Texas homeowners can tell hail damage from normal wear, what to expect from the adjuster, how to avoid contractor scams and what to do if the insurer underpays.</p>

<h2>Texas Hail Season and Why Roofs Take the Hit</h2>
<p>Texas hail season commonly peaks in spring, when severe thunderstorms are most frequent, though damaging hail can fall in other months too. A single fast-moving storm can damage roofs across an entire metro area like Houston, Dallas or San Antonio in minutes, and every affected homeowner ends up calling the same insurers and roofers at once.</p>
<p>Roofs absorb the worst of it because hail strikes them directly. Damage is often invisible from the ground, which is why many homeowners do not realize they have a claim until a leak shows up months later. By then, the insurer may question whether the damage came from that storm.</p>
<p>After any significant hailstorm, note the date, keep a photo of hail on the ground with something for scale, and schedule a roof inspection from a reputable, licensed contractor. A NOAA weather radio also helps you track when storms hit your area.</p>
<p>[[product:midland-weather-radio]]</p>

<h2>What Hail Damage Looks Like vs. Normal Wear</h2>
<p>Adjusters and roofers look for specific signs. Knowing them helps you understand the inspection and push back when something is missed.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Sign</th><th>Likely hail damage</th><th>Likely wear or other cause</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Granule loss</td><td>Random, circular spots where granules were knocked off, often exposing dark asphalt</td><td>Even granule loss across the roof, granules collecting in gutters over years</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bruising</td><td>Soft spots in shingles that feel like a bruised apple when pressed</td><td>Not present with normal aging</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cracks and fractures</td><td>Fresh, random fractures in shingles or tiles</td><td>Cracking and curling from heat, age or poor ventilation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pattern</td><td>Random hits, heavier on slopes facing the storm</td><td>Uniform deterioration or damage in one area from foot traffic or debris</td></tr>
<tr><td>Collateral damage</td><td>Dents in gutters, vents, AC fins, window screens and soft metals</td><td>No matching dents on surrounding surfaces</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Collateral damage matters. Dented gutters, downspouts and roof vents help show that hail actually hit the property and how large it was.</p>

<h3>Functional vs. cosmetic damage</h3>
<p>Functional damage affects the roof's ability to shed water or shortens its life, such as fractured shingles or punctured metal. Cosmetic damage changes appearance without affecting function, such as shallow dents in metal panels. Many disputes come down to which category the adjuster puts your roof in.</p>

<h2>Policy Fine Print That Shapes Hail Claims</h2>
<p>Before filing, read three parts of your policy: endorsements on the roof, the wind and hail deductible, and how the roof is valued.</p>

<h3>Cosmetic damage exclusions</h3>
<p>Some policies exclude cosmetic hail damage to roof coverings through an endorsement, sometimes tied to a discount for an impact-resistant roof. If your policy has one, dents that do not let water in may not be paid. If the roof has both cosmetic and functional damage, the functional part is still a covered claim.</p>

<h3>Actual cash value roof endorsements</h3>
<p>TDI explains that replacement cost coverage can pay up to the full cost to repair your roof at current prices, while actual cash value coverage pays less when a roof is older or worn. TDI's <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/tips/replacing-your-roof.html">roof replacement tips</a> note that companies may switch roofs from replacement cost to actual cash value as they age, and that your company should tell you when it changes your coverage. On an older roof, depreciation can shrink the check considerably.</p>

<h3>Wind and hail percentage deductibles</h3>
<p>Many Texas policies use a separate deductible for wind and hail, often a percentage of your dwelling coverage limit rather than a flat dollar amount. A 2% deductible on a home insured for $350,000 is $7,000, regardless of the size of the roof bill. Our <a href="/blog/hurricane-damage-insurance-claim-texas/">hurricane claim guide</a> includes a table of how percentage deductibles work at different coverage levels.</p>

<h2>Texas Law: No Contractor May Waive Your Deductible</h2>
<p>A roofer offering a "free roof" or promising to "take care of your deductible" is offering something illegal in Texas.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Texas Insurance Code section 707.002</strong> requires a person insured under a property insurance policy to pay any deductible on a first-party claim.</li>
<li><strong>Texas Business and Commerce Code section 27.02</strong> makes it an offense for a seller of goods or services paid from property insurance proceeds to pay, waive, absorb or fail to collect the deductible, or to give a rebate or credit that offsets it, without the insurer's consent. It is a Class B misdemeanor.</li>
<li>The same section requires contracts of $1,000 or more expected to be paid from a property insurance claim to include a notice, in at least 12-point bold type, that Texas law requires the insured to pay any deductible.</li>
</ul>
<p>Inflating an estimate to cover the deductible creates problems for you as well as the contractor. Pay your deductible, and walk away from anyone who offers otherwise.</p>

<h2>Storm Chasers and Contractor Red Flags</h2>
<p>After big hail events, out-of-area crews knock on doors across Houston and the rest of Texas. Some are legitimate; many are not.</p>
<ul>
<li>Pressure to sign a contract on the spot, especially before the adjuster inspects</li>
<li>Offers to waive or cover your deductible</li>
<li>Requests for large upfront payments in cash</li>
<li>No local address, references or verifiable insurance</li>
<li>Promises to "handle your claim" or negotiate with your insurer for you</li>
</ul>
<p>That last one matters legally. Under Texas Insurance Code section 4102.163, a contractor may not act as a public adjuster or advertise to adjust claims on any property where it is providing or may provide contracting services, even if it holds an adjuster license. A roofer can document damage and meet the adjuster; negotiating your claim is a job for you, a licensed public adjuster or an attorney.</p>

<h2>Filing the Claim and the Adjuster Inspection</h2>
<p>The general claim process applies: protect the property, document, read your policy and report promptly. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-file-a-home-insurance-claim/">how to file a home insurance claim</a> covers it in detail, including Texas payment deadlines. For roofs specifically:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Tarp any openings</strong> to prevent interior water damage, and keep the receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph collateral damage</strong> on gutters, vents, AC units, screens, fences and vehicles.</li>
<li><strong>Get a roofer's inspection</strong> with dated photos of marked hail hits on each slope.</li>
<li><strong>Be present at the adjuster inspection,</strong> ideally with your roofer, and ask the adjuster to check every slope.</li>
<li><strong>Request the estimate in writing</strong> and compare it with your roofer's scope.</li>
</ol>
<p>[[product:heavy-duty-tarp]]</p>

<h3>Matching problems</h3>
<p>If only some slopes are damaged, the insurer may pay to repair those sections. When replacement shingles no longer match in color or style, homeowners often argue the whole roof must be replaced. How matching is handled depends on your policy wording and the facts, so ask the insurer in writing how it addressed matching and why. A roofer's written note that the existing shingles are discontinued or cannot be matched is useful evidence to include.</p>

<h2>If the Insurer Underpays or Denies the Roof</h2>
<p>Start by asking for a re-inspection, ideally with a different adjuster and your roofer present. Send photos of hail hits, collateral damage and your roofer's written findings.</p>
<p>If you agree the roof was damaged by hail but disagree on how much it costs to fix, the appraisal clause may help. In <em>State Farm Lloyds v. Johnson</em> (2009), a hail roof case, the Texas Supreme Court held that the homeowner could invoke appraisal even though the dispute over whether to repair part of the roof or replace all of it involved questions of what the hail caused. Our guide to the <a href="/blog/appraisal-clause-insurance/">appraisal clause</a> explains how the process works and the new Texas rules taking effect for policies issued or renewed on or after March 1, 2027.</p>
<p>Other options include filing a complaint with TDI, hiring a licensed public adjuster or consulting an attorney. Hail is a weather claim, so Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542A generally requires written notice at least 61 days before filing suit. For a large or denied roof claim, confirm your options with a licensed Texas insurance attorney or public adjuster.</p>

<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>A strong hail damage roof insurance claim rests on proof of functional damage, prompt reporting and a clear read of your policy's cosmetic, actual cash value and deductible terms. Pay your own deductible, avoid contractors who offer to negotiate for you, document collateral damage and use re-inspection or appraisal when the numbers do not add up.</p>
